Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage for determining the highest‑run partnerships in a match  

• ball_by_ball  
  – "match_id": groups deliveries by match  
  – "innings_no": distinguishes the two innings of a match  
  – "over_id", "ball_id": allow perfect alignment with the other delivery–level tables  
  – "striker", "non_striker": the two batter‑ids at the crease on every delivery (i.e., the prospective partnership pair)  

• batsman_scored  
  – "match_id", "innings_no", "over_id", "ball_id": join keys to ball_by_ball  
  – "runs_scored": runs credited to the striker on each ball; summing this after a join gives partnership runs (extras are ignored for pure batter partnership runs)  

• wicket_taken  
  – "match_id", "innings_no", "over_id", "ball_id": lets us mark where a partnership ends (player_out ≠ NULL)  
  – "player_out": helps identify which batter was dismissed (not yet needed for the exploratory queries below, but indispensable later for precise partnership boundaries)  

• player  
  – "player_id", "player_name": optional if we want to display names instead of IDs  

Typical conditions that will be used later  
1. Join ball_by_ball ↔ batsman_scored ON (match_id, innings_no, over_id, ball_id) to fetch the striker/non‑striker pair together with striker’s runs.  
2. Optional WHERE batsman_scored.runs_scored IS NOT NULL to keep only valid scoring deliveries.  
3. A CASE expression (or MIN/MAX) to order the two player IDs so that the smaller/larger ID can be treated consistently when aggregating an unordered pair.  
4. GROUP BY match_id, innings_no, ordered_player1, ordered_player2 to sum runs for every partnership stint.  
5. Find the maximum summed runs per match and keep all ties.  
6. When two partners have equal individual contribution, use the higher player_id as player 1 (this can be done with CASE in ORDER BY).  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (all SELECT statements, each limited to 20 rows) progressing from simple inspection to more complex aggregations.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at 20 random rows from ball_by_ball to see striker/non_striker values.
SELECT *
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: List distinct striker IDs appearing in ball_by_ball.
SELECT DISTINCT striker
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: List distinct non_striker IDs appearing in ball_by_ball.
SELECT DISTINCT non_striker
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Inspect batsman_scored rows; confirm we have one record per delivery.
SELECT *
FROM batsman_scored
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Join ball_by_ball with batsman_scored for one arbitrary match to see how they align.
SELECT b."match_id",
       b."over_id",
       b."ball_id",
       b."striker",
       b."non_striker",
       s."runs_scored"
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b."match_id"   = s."match_id"
 AND b."innings_no" = s."innings_no"
 AND b."over_id"    = s."over_id"
 AND b."ball_id"    = s."ball_id"
WHERE b."match_id" = 335987            -- example match_id from sample data
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Compute total runs scored by each striker in one match (both innings combined).
SELECT b."match_id",
       b."striker"          AS player_id,
       SUM(s."runs_scored") AS total_striker_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b."match_id"   = s."match_id"
 AND b."innings_no" = s."innings_no"
 AND b."over_id"    = s."over_id"
 AND b."ball_id"    = s."ball_id"
WHERE b."match_id" = 335987
GROUP BY b."match_id", b."striker"
ORDER BY total_striker_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Show all distinct striker/non_striker unordered pairs for one match.
SELECT b."match_id",
       CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."striker" ELSE b."non_striker" END AS player_a,
       CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."non_striker" ELSE b."striker" END AS player_b
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
WHERE b."match_id" = 335987
GROUP BY b."match_id", player_a, player_b
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Sum partnership runs for every unordered pair inside one match (ignoring extras).
SELECT b."match_id",
       CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."striker" ELSE b."non_striker" END AS player_a,
       CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."non_striker" ELSE b."striker" END AS player_b,
       SUM(s."runs_scored") AS partnership_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b."match_id"   = s."match_id"
 AND b."innings_no" = s."innings_no"
 AND b."over_id"    = s."over_id"
 AND b."ball_id"    = s."ball_id"
WHERE b."match_id" = 335987
GROUP BY b."match_id", player_a, player_b
ORDER BY partnership_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Same as #8 but across all matches; shows top 20 partnership totals overall.
SELECT b."match_id",
       CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."striker" ELSE b."non_striker" END AS player_a,
       CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."non_striker" ELSE b."striker" END AS player_b,
       SUM(s."runs_scored") AS partnership_runs
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS s
  ON b."match_id"   = s."match_id"
 AND b."innings_no" = s."innings_no"
 AND b."over_id"    = s."over_id"
 AND b."ball_id"    = s."ball_id"
GROUP BY b."match_id", player_a, player_b
ORDER BY partnership_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10. Description: For every match, pick the partnership(s) having the highest summed runs and keep ties.
-- (Uses a correlated sub‑query to compare each partnership’s runs with the match’s max.)
SELECT t."match_id",
       t."player_a" AS player1_id,
       t."player_b" AS player2_id,
       t."partnership_runs"
FROM (
    SELECT b."match_id",
           CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."striker" ELSE b."non_striker" END AS player_a,
           CASE WHEN b."striker" < b."non_striker" THEN b."non_striker" ELSE b."striker" END AS player_b,
           SUM(s."runs_scored") AS partnership_runs
    FROM ball_by_ball AS b
    JOIN batsman_scored AS s
      ON b."match_id"   = s."match_id"
     AND b."innings_no" = s."innings_no"
     AND b."over_id"    = s."over_id"
     AND b."ball_id"    = s."ball_id"
    GROUP BY b."match_id", player_a, player_b
) AS t
WHERE t."partnership_runs" = (
      SELECT MAX(u."partnership_runs")
      FROM (
          SELECT CASE WHEN b2."striker" < b2."non_striker" THEN b2."striker" ELSE b2."non_striker" END AS p1,
                 CASE WHEN b2."striker" < b2."non_striker" THEN b2."non_striker" ELSE b2."striker" END AS p2,
                 SUM(s2."runs_scored") AS partnership_runs
          FROM ball_by_ball AS b2
          JOIN batsman_scored AS s2
            ON b2."match_id"   = s2."match_id"
           AND b2."innings_no" = s2."innings_no"
           AND b2."over_id"    = s2."over_id"
           AND b2."ball_id"    = s2."ball_id"
          WHERE b2."match_id" = t."match_id"
          GROUP BY p1, p2
      ) AS u
)
ORDER BY t."match_id", t."partnership_runs" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

The last query (#10) is a draft of the ultimate logic needed for the problem statement; it still ignores (a) splitting partnerships at wickets and (b) the tie‑breaking rule where the higher‑scoring individual batter should be labeled player 1. Those refinements can be layered on top of the same general aggregation structure.
